---
interface Props { top?: boolean }
// On its own page the case title is the h1 and each beat label an h2; inside
// the one-page version they sit one level lower. Beat labels are headings so
// screen-reader users can jump beat to beat.
const { top = false } = Astro.props;
const HT = top ? 'h1' : 'h2';
const HL = top ? 'h2' : 'h3';
import Pic from './Pic.astro';
import Split from './Split.astro';
import CaseContents from './CaseContents.astro';
import BrandMark from './BrandMark.astro';
import BrandSystem from './BrandSystem.astro';
import DecisionMap from './dg/AuthoredDecisions.astro';
import AuthoredPersonas from './dg/AuthoredPersonas.astro';
import AuthoredJourney from './dg/AuthoredJourney.astro';
import CoParent from './dg/CoParent.astro';
import WinkingScreens from './WinkingScreens.astro';
import ProductCapture from './ProductCapture.astro';
import Flow from './dg/Flow.astro';

---
<section id="winking" data-tone="light" class="ws band">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="chapter-head">
      <div class="g" style="row-gap:28px;align-items:end">
        <div style="grid-column:1/9;display:grid;gap:24px">
          <p class="eyebrow">Case three · Solo · web and iOS</p>
          <BrandMark project="winking" />
          <HT class="h-chapter" style="view-transition-name:t-winking">Winking Star</HT>
        </div>
        <p class="lead muted" style="grid-column:1/9">A shared routine board that a child can make their own. A tap becomes a star, the week grows a Mystery Pet, and another adult can pick up where the family left off. I designed and built the web and iPhone experiences myself.</p>
        <div class="meta" style="grid-column:1/13"><span><b>Role</b> · Designer and builder, one of one</span><span><b>Platforms</b> · Web, iPhone and iPad</span><span><b>Status</b> · Live web app and iOS v1.0.4</span><span><b>Languages</b> · EN, FR, ES, DE, Traditional Chinese</span></div>
        <p class="ws-product-links"><a href="https://winkingstar.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open Winking Star<span class="vh"> (opens in new tab)</span></a><a href="https://apps.apple.com/app/id6765767262" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View the iPhone app<span class="vh"> (opens in new tab)</span></a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="project-hero ws-product-hero">
        <ProductCapture name="/art/winking-hero-composition.svg" alt="Winking Star iPhone Home, Activity and Progress screens in the original staggered three-phone composition" href="https://winkingstar.com/" label="Original Winking Star iPhone composition" eager />
      </div>
    </header>
    <CaseContents project="winking" />

    <div class="beat wide rv" id="ws-hero">
      <div class="body">
        <div class="ws-hero">
          <div class="art" aria-hidden="true"><Pic name="ws-hero-art.jpg" alt="" sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 60vw" /></div>
          <div class="copy">
            <HL>Tiny routines, happy families</HL>
            <p>I started with the moment after a routine is done. A child taps, sees a star, and has a reason to come back tomorrow. The pet, the theme and the weekly reward make progress visible without turning a missed day into a failure.</p>
            <div class="chips"><span class="chip">No ads, ever</span><span class="chip">No tracking</span><span class="chip">Up to 6 kids</span><span class="chip">Co-parents on one board</span></div>
          </div>
          <div aria-hidden="true"></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="beat rv" id="ws-problem">
      <div class="label"><HL class="k"><b>01</b> Problem and research</HL></div>
      <div class="body">
        <Split as="h3" class="h-sub" text="Bedtime in our house was a nag loop. Every chore chart we tried was built for adults." />
        <ul class="rows">
          <li><b>Problem</b><p>Teeth, bath, reading, Mandarin practice, out of the door for a walk. Two children, two adults, and no shared view of what was done. The kids stopped looking at every chart within a week.</p></li>
          <li><b>Research</b><p>I built it for my own two kids and watched them use it each evening. I explored big icons, stickers and celebration moments to give them a reason to look at the board. Naming research too. Sparkle and Twinkle were taken, so it became Winking Star.</p></li>
          <li><b>Goal and success measure</b><p>A child who asks to tick their own stars, and a parent who opens it the next evening without being reminded. Routines completed per week, weeks retained, and whether a second adult joins the board.</p></li>
          <li><b>Constraints</b><p>Solo, evenings and weekends. Free to start with a one-off lifetime unlock, no ads and no subscription, because it's for children. Kids Category rules on the App Store shaped the parental gate and the privacy manifest.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="beat rv" id="ws-personas">
      <div class="label"><HL class="k"><b>02</b> Personas</HL></div>
      <div class="body">
        <Split as="h3" class="h-sub" text="Three users, one star. Nobody has the same job." />
        <AuthoredPersonas project="winking" id="winking-personas" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="art-note">Role illustrations, not photographs of research participants.</p>
    <div class="beat rv" id="ws-flow">
      <div class="label"><HL class="k"><b>03</b> The full flow</HL></div>
      <div class="body">
        <Split as="h3" class="h-sub" text="One family board. Two ways to use it." />
        <p>I keep the same job across web and iPhone: find your child, mark the routine, see what changed. The web puts the week on one board. The iPhone app separates Home, Activity, Treasure and Progress into focused tabs.</p>
        <Flow label="The current Winking Star family workflow" steps={[
          {t:'Choose a superstar',d:'Their name, avatar and theme make the board recognisable.'},
          {t:'Mark the routine',d:'Tap the activity for the right day. A sticker shows it is done.'},
          {t:'See the week grow',d:'Stars connect the small daily action to a pet and a family reward.'},
          {t:'Share the board',d:'A second adult joins the same family, with the same routines.'},
        ]}/>
        <WinkingScreens />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="beat rv" id="ws-journey">
      <div class="label"><HL class="k"><b>04</b> Journey</HL></div>
      <div class="body">
        <Split as="h3" class="h-sub" text="From a bad bedtime to week two." />
        <p>The original iPhone journey follows the Sample Family through the first tick, the weekly pet and a second adult joining. The web keeps those motivations, with the weekly chart on one page and a separate setup flow.</p>
        <AuthoredJourney project="winking" id="winking-journey" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="beat rv" id="ws-loops">
      <div class="label"><HL class="k"><b>05</b> Loops and decisions</HL></div>
      <div class="body">
        <Split as="h3" class="h-sub" text="Two loops and six decisions." />
        <p>These are the decisions behind the iPhone app. The web carries the same routines, themes and family data into a weekly board. I keep the daily action short and let the week give it meaning.</p>
        <div>
          <div class="loop-label">Daily · a short routine</div>
          <div class="loop"><span>Open</span><i>→</i><span>Pick your child</span><i>→</i><span>Tap a star spot</span><i>→</i><span>Star lands</span><i>→</i><span>Celebration</span><i>→</i><span class="end">Pet grows a little</span></div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="loop-label">Weekly · the reason to come back</div>
          <div class="loop"><span>Seven days of stars</span><i>→</i><span>Star-based pet growth</span><i>→</i><span>Treasures and badges</span><i>→</i><span>Fridge summary</span><i>→</i><span class="end">Monday, a new pet</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="demo" id="demo">
          <div class="demo-head"><span class="demo-name"><Pic name="ws-icon.png" alt="" sizes="40px" widths={[80]} />Try it · Leo, Tuesday</span><span class="demo-stars" id="demo-stars" aria-live="polite">0 of 6 stars</span></div>
          <div class="demo-routines" role="group" aria-label="Tick a routine">
            <button type="button" class="rt" aria-pressed="false"><span class="em" aria-hidden="true">🦷</span>Teeth</button>
            <button type="button" class="rt" aria-pressed="false"><span class="em" aria-hidden="true">🛁</span>Bath</button>
            <button type="button" class="rt" aria-pressed="false"><span class="em" aria-hidden="true">📖</span>Reading</button>
            <button type="button" class="rt" aria-pressed="false"><span class="em" aria-hidden="true">🀄</span>Mandarin</button>
            <button type="button" class="rt" aria-pressed="false"><span class="em" aria-hidden="true">🚶</span>Walk</button>
            <button type="button" class="rt" aria-pressed="false"><span class="em" aria-hidden="true">😴</span>Sleep</button>
          </div>
          <div class="demo-pet">
            <div class="egg" id="egg" aria-hidden="true"><div class="fill" id="egg-fill"></div><span class="pet" id="egg-pet">🥚</span></div>
            <div style="display:grid;gap:10px">
              <div class="stages" id="stages" aria-hidden="true"><span class="on">Egg</span><span>Hatching</span><span>Growing</span><span>Grown</span></div>
              <p class="muted" id="demo-msg">This is a simplified four-stage illustration. The current app grows the pet at star thresholds across the week. Tick all six routines here to preview the idea.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <DecisionMap project="winking" />

      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="beat wide rv"><div class="body"><BrandSystem project="winking" /></div></div>

    <div class="beat wide rv" id="ws-themes">
      <div class="body">
        <div class="theme-gallery" role="region" aria-label="Theme banners from the app">
          <Pic name="ws-source-football.png" alt="Football theme banner" sizes="(max-width: 640px) 90vw, 45vw" widths={[480, 800]} /><Pic name="ws-source-dinosaur.png" alt="Dinosaur theme banner" sizes="(max-width: 640px) 90vw, 45vw" widths={[480, 800]} /><Pic name="ws-source-ocean.png" alt="Ocean theme banner" sizes="(max-width: 640px) 90vw, 45vw" widths={[480, 800]} /><Pic name="ws-source-unicorn.png" alt="Unicorn theme banner" sizes="(max-width: 640px) 90vw, 45vw" widths={[480, 800]} />
        </div>
        <p class="cap">The original theme artwork is shared across web and iPhone. A familiar scene helps a child recognise their own space before they read a label.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="beat rv" id="ws-edge">
      <div class="label"><HL class="k"><b>06</b> Edge cases and states</HL></div>
      <div class="body">
        <Split as="h3" class="h-sub" text="The iPhone states beyond the happy path." />
        <ul class="rows two">
          <li><b>First run, no family yet</b><p>The iPhone app offers “Try the Sample Family” alongside family creation, so a parent can see the loop before setting up their own board. On the web, a parent chooses a theme and completes a grown-up check before adding child details. Those are different entry points, so the case study shows them separately.</p></li>
          <li><b>A missed routine, or a missed day</b><p>Stars never go negative and nothing turns red. The day just has fewer stars. The pet still grows on what was done.</p></li>
          <li><b>Midnight</b><p>The day rolls over at local midnight even with the app open, so a late tick lands on the right day. That one shipped as a fix in 1.0.4.</p></li>
          <li><b>Offline in the bathroom</b><p>An offline banner, ticks queued, synced on reconnect. Two adults can tick the same kid from two phones.</p></li>
          <li><b>Split homes and birthdays</b><p>A custody label, "at Mum's this week", so the board reads right in both houses. A birthday banner for the whole birthday week.</p></li>
          <li><b>Accessibility</b><p>Tabs expose their labels and selected state to VoiceOver. Text supports Dynamic Type up to 1.6×, and confetti stays off when Reduce Motion or Calm Mode is enabled.</p></li>
        </ul>
        <CoParent />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="beat rv" id="ws-outcome">
      <div class="label"><HL class="k"><b>07</b> Outcome</HL></div>
      <div class="body">
        <Split as="h3" class="h-sub" text="A live product, shaped by the family using it." />
        <div class="metrics">
          <div class="metric"><span class="big w">1.0.4</span><span>iOS release, alongside the live web board</span></div>
          <div class="metric"><span class="big" data-count="5">5</span><span>languages. English, French, Spanish, German and Traditional Chinese</span></div>
          <!-- DECISION: no measured download figure yet, so the tile shows a fact the page already states ("Four releases so far"). Swap back when App Store Connect numbers are in. -->
          <div class="metric"><span class="big" data-count="4">4</span><span>releases since launch, all on the App Store</span></div>
        </div>
        <ul class="rows">
          <li><b>What shipped after launch</b><p>Four releases so far. Privacy and safety work for the Kids Category, smoother weekly stars and treasures, the midnight rollover fix, VoiceOver improvements, faster startup, and rating from Settings.</p></li>
          <li><b>What I'd change next</b><p>I would test the handoff between the web setup and the first useful board, then watch a second adult join without help. I also want the small-screen chart and pet helper text to remain readable across all five languages. These are the next checks, not measured results.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
.ws-product-links{grid-column:1/-1;display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:16px 28px;font-size:15px}
.ws-product-links a{text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:5px}
.ws-product-hero{max-width:1100px;margin-inline:auto;background:transparent;padding:0}
.ws-product-hero :global(img){display:block;width:100%;height:auto;border:0;border-radius:4px}
</style>
